  "narrative": {
    "text": "**Business & market dynamics:** The company’s revenue base has contracted sharply following the consensual foreclosure of a key Manhattan asset, leaving a leaner five-property portfolio concentrated in transit-rich New York City locations. The remaining tenant roster is notably stable, with a high share of investment-grade or implied-investment-grade names and extended lease maturities—60% of leases now stretch beyond 2030—limiting near-term rollover risk. While the broader NYC office market faces headwinds, the portfolio’s credit quality and location-driven demand appear to be supporting occupancy and retention in a downsized, more defensive posture.\n\n**Management strategy and execution:** Leadership has pivoted to portfolio rationalization, shedding troubled assets to stabilize the business. The emphasis now falls on cost reductions and proactive lease extensions to protect cash flow, rather than pursuing growth. Management points to the growing share of long-dated leases and creditworthy tenants as evidence that the strategy is taking hold, though the tone remains cautious. Communication is steady but offers no new expansion initiatives, framing the company as a stabilized, income-oriented vehicle working through the aftermath of significant asset losses.",
